---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Sustainability"
	description="Sustainability on Irish golf courses: resource-efficient machinery, water management, and course resilience - practical measures, not slogans."
>
	<section class="section section--tight hero">
		<div class="container">
			<span class="eyebrow">Sustainability</span>
			<h1>Sustainability that pays for itself</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				On a busy golf course, sustainability isn't a separate initiative; it's fuel, labour,
				water, and materials used more efficiently, and habitats protected and improved. The
				measures below are chosen because they pay for themselves in reduced costs, extended
				playing days, and the potential for increased green fee income.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="grid grid--2">
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Sustainable Drainage</h3>
					<p>
						Drainage is usually the single most effective investment a course can make. It's
						where most of my experience lies and the core of much of the advice given on this
						website. As well as extending the playing season, proper drainage reduces
						compaction, cuts the risk of surface damage, and lowers the cost of repairs after
						every wet winter. My approach is always to refresh and repair the existing system
						with the minimum intervention needed, rather than starting from scratch, because
						that's usually both the most sustainable option and the one that pays for itself
						fastest. Done well, it is effective, long lasting, and easily upgraded over time.
					</p>
					<a href="/drainage/">See the Drainage programme →</a>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Robotic mowing</h3>
					<p>
						Robotic mowers are already changing how golf courses are maintained. Battery-powered
						and near-silent, they can work early or late without disturbing golfers, and cause
						far less compaction than a diesel ride-on. The clubs who have already embraced
						robotic mowing are seeing great benefits in turf quality, year-round cutting, reduced
						fairway damage, and staff time released for other important work. Most clubs are
						starting with the softer, wetter areas where robots do the most good, while
						conventional machines still cover the bulk of fairway mowing, for now. That's likely
						just the beginning. The business case is covered in detail in our Technical Notes.
					</p>
					<a href="/technical-notes/">Read the Technical Notes →</a>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Surveying &amp; course mapping</h3>
					<p>
						Accurate drainage and course maps mean interventions are targeted rather than
						speculative, less material, less machinery time, and a permanent record for future
						work.
					</p>
					<a href="/services/surveys-maps/">See Surveys &amp; Maps →</a>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Climate resilience</h3>
					<p>
						Irish winters are getting wetter, with heavier rain falling more often. Courses
						built decades ago weren't designed for today's rainfall, so resilience means
						updating drainage and maintenance systems to cope with the conditions we actually
						have now.
					</p>
					<a href="/contact/">Talk through your course →</a>
				</div>
				<figure style="grid-column: span 2; margin:0;">
					<img
						src="/images/gallery/best-of-both-worlds-3.png"
						alt="Robotic and traditional reel mowers working together on a fairway"
						style="width:100%; height:auto; border-radius:8px; box-shadow: var(--shadow);"
					/>
					<figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem; font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--color-body);">
						Robotic mowers and traditional reel mowers working together to improve fairway condition.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container">
			<span class="eyebrow">In practice</span>
			<h2>What this looks like on the ground</h2>
			<div class="grid grid--3">
				<figure style="margin:0;">
					<img
						src="/images/gallery/rainfall-trends.jpg"
						alt="Chart showing the trend of winter rainfall over the last 15 years"
						style="width:100%; height:14rem; object-fit:cover; border-radius:8px;"
					/>
					<figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem; font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--color-body);">
						Winter rainfall trends over the last 15 years, some up, some down, but the trend is clear.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure style="margin:0;">
					<img
						src="/images/gallery/spring-again.jpg"
						alt="A healthy fairway framed by cherry trees in full bloom"
						style="width:100%; height:14rem; object-fit:cover; border-radius:8px;"
					/>
					<figcaption style="margin-top:0.5rem; font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--color-body);">
						A course that drains and recovers well stays healthy and playable through the seasons.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section--alt">
		<div class="container" style="display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:1.5rem; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between;">
			<div>
				<h2 style="margin-top:0;">Where to start</h2>
				<p style="margin-bottom:0; max-width:34rem;">
					Most courses get the biggest early win from drainage, it's the fastest way to extend
					the playing season and reduce downstream costs. A free consultation identifies the
					priority order for your course.
				</p>
			</div>
			<a class="btn btn--primary" href="/contact/">Book a consultation</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
